  [1mabout"[0m (a subscription or ownership relation), and [1mchange detection[0m (something that observes a transition and emits an
  event). Notifications are the last layer on that stack, not the first.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this is one project or two. If the task layer doesn't exist yet, a notifications spec written now
  would be inventing the task model by implication — every event shape and subscription key would be a guess about a
  schema nobody has agreed to, and re-deciding that later means rewriting the notification layer too. That's the
  expensive-to-change direction. Conversely, if tasks already live somewhere I haven't seen, I should read that first
  and design against the real schema.

  - [1mTasks first, then notifications[0m — two specs, two cycles. Costs an extra round trip before any notification code
    exists; buys a real schema to design events against. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mPoint me at the existing app[0m — if the tasks live in another repo or behind an API, notifications stay a single
    well-scoped project. Fastest path if it exists.
  - [1mThin task layer inside this spec[0m — I define the minimum task shape needed to demo notifications. Fast, but the task
    model gets designed as a side effect of the notification design, which is backwards and hard to undo.
  - [1mFull greenfield: tasks + users + notifications[0m — honest about the real scope, but that's three subsystems in one
    spec; the skill's own guidance says decompose rather than refine details of something that needs splitting.

  My recommendation is the first: establish tasks (and whatever "user" means here) as its own small spec, then
  brainstorm notifications against a schema that actually exists. But if there's an app I haven't seen, the second
  option collapses all of this.
